#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188063 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37d7e5c8-9b77-5eb1-beb8-4355a5a9d625" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Elements of a Covariance Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "c72f3cc2-5851-5c6b-8edc-74377b88055a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10433" :question/problem "![](images/21/21780b1ef6e634615275f2ae4681ac6722398e7400a02a3cb9daf07b8b407da8.svg)\n\nThe covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ for some random variables $X_1, X_2,$ and $X_3$ is given above. Find $a + b + c.$" :question/worked-solution "For random variables $X_1, \\ldots, X_n,$ the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n\n![](images/4a/4a9170c03d62e3946d5c825c9baa688fcbcb35247ff123764de3970635977a8d.svg)\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is $\\Sigma_{ii} = \\textrm{Var}[X_i],$ and\n\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i \\neq j$ is $\\Sigma_{ij} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_i, X_j].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a = \\Sigma_{21} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_2,X_1] = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2] = \\Sigma_{12} = 2$\n\n- $b = \\Sigma_{23} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_2,X_3] = \\textrm{Cov}[X_3,X_2] = \\Sigma_{32} = 1$\n\n- $c = \\Sigma_{31} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_3,X_1] = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_3] = \\Sigma_{13} = 0$\n\nTherefore, $a + b + c = 2 + 1 +0 = 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188070 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46118198-5e82-5b8f-8adc-6f25ecbd9b3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given a Discrete Bivariate Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "5b315157-43c6-5b8e-ac70-8b781b41cec9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10452" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n\nGiven that $\\textrm{E}[X] = 3$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y] = 2.2,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n![](images/c4/c457df4f5450340a178e0f8c951ece5643acb78295bd98fe935d0a81933a3112.svg)\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2,\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.5$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\textrm{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support $S_X,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =10-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support $S_Y,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =5.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =5.8-{2.2}^{2} \\\\  & =0.96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\textrm{E}[XY]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2)(1)f(2,1)+(4)(1)f(4,1)+(2)(3)f(2,3)+(4)(3)f(4,3) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.3+4\\cdot 0.1+6\\cdot 0.2+12\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =7-3\\cdot 2.2 \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n![](images/27/27afb746d2ec171983cfa7b06d5f4f2c00909a92ac44abc5890b67d84f6eb5e9.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188071 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2c2ed97-706b-565d-997e-39bd0c832726" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given Some Moments"}] :question/id #uuid "33336c58-c83a-512f-8a79-9223d1442763" :question/math-academy-id "e-10453" :question/problem "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\textrm{E}[X] = 4,$ $\\textrm{E}[Y] = 3,$ mixed moment $\\textrm{E}[XY] = 9,$ and second raw moments $\\textrm{E}[X^2] = 18$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y^2] = 14.$ Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n\n![](images/4a/4a6aa6958f9b91163fba81f9a29f32d245af5902be44313efc786a2bf4da6099.svg)\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2,\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\textrm{Var}[X]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =18-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =14-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow lets find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =9-4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n![](images/b9/b9d86366c14e62d805d7e046f27ef40cc572048ec7e9f868b6cf31cea94ee7a4.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188072 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188364 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e65da0e-4de3-5292-aaef-c646771ec0f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given a Continuous Bivariate Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "3a22ccc6-495b-5b89-8b7d-775e6e620fe0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10454" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n![](images/4a/4a0e63105483b697842963df430c5341abef68b83501e9d63d17653b88309c59.svg)\n\nIf $\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{7}{12},$ $\\textrm{E}[Y] = \\dfrac{5}{8},$ and $\\textrm{E}[XY]=\\dfrac{17}{48},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n\n![](images/e7/e7e614e58780e1600532e0cd172674c921feaae0ff35b47a01d0befaa66230ac.svg)\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2,\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{Var}[X].$\nWe compute $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}y+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-{(\\frac{7}{12})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\textrm{E}[Y^2]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{4})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x{y}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{4})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}+\\frac{3}{10}{y}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{15}-{(\\frac{5}{8})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{73}{960}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].$ Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{48}-(\\frac{7}{12})\\cdot (\\frac{5}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{96}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n![](images/a1/a16a9b39d5ff75935e7394d1ff0f3eb03437bc000038b20925d1f91985386b48.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2c2ed97-706b-565d-997e-39bd0c832726" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given Some Moments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42f26747-e73f-585c-ac6e-199a99404cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547464} {:answer/id #uuid "64529e3f-0c34-5f86-9d4f-48706fda59b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547538} {:answer/id #uuid "986fdf6a-6bc1-5fff-82d4-52582eabab4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547649} {:answer/id #uuid "bf58e07d-f149-57e2-8a07-3c1b1f8703a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547727} {:answer/id #uuid "fa3e1fb2-59d9-5c07-b6fb-3e6cd3182503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42f26747-e73f-585c-ac6e-199a99404cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547464} :answer-field/id #uuid "4804197d-04ff-5c8c-8cdf-466d734495d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29c433e1-ba8d-5d7e-b983-459746ebf86b" :question/math-academy-id "q-148179" :question/problem "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\text{E}[X]=2,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=4,$ mixed moment $\\text{E}[XY]=10,$ and second raw moments $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=9$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=20.$ Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\text{Var}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =9-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =20-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow lets find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =10-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188364 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e65da0e-4de3-5292-aaef-c646771ec0f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given a Continuous Bivariate Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01df44a6-657a-5670-a985-4a87b7716922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547320} {:answer/id #uuid "0a71cefc-8de5-5de6-a1e7-e827ad772d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547337} {:answer/id #uuid "2529513a-9fa9-5a66-b66c-95b167283ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547386} {:answer/id #uuid "b1914b83-6794-52fe-91db-700c3ef7d55d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547703} {:answer/id #uuid "ef02e2b8-d181-5dc2-952d-5f459a779602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a71cefc-8de5-5de6-a1e7-e827ad772d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547337} :answer-field/id #uuid "f13c53ef-1e0a-55f0-86d4-e8d2d1425533" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "150d719c-c9e6-5c71-9c3c-1c207b1e3bd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-148189" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{7}{12},$ and $E[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{5}{12},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\text{Var}[X].$ Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-{(\\frac{7}{12})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}-{(\\frac{7}{12})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-(\\frac{7}{12})\\cdot (\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{144} & -\\frac{1}{144} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{144} & \\frac{11}{144}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 11\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37d7e5c8-9b77-5eb1-beb8-4355a5a9d625" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Elements of a Covariance Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a7263a0-4076-5c8a-bf2b-7ef8f639c709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547400} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad50d19-89cd-5b90-93fc-253e38284494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547519} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf6a545-5662-5239-8a46-31ba0f543614" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547622} {:answer/id #uuid "b5245d4f-7082-5752-b664-dc2f204a93ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547709} {:answer/id #uuid "c21d0edf-d85d-510d-8c4b-8c82083f83db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ad50d19-89cd-5b90-93fc-253e38284494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547519} :answer-field/id #uuid "18ece77b-cabe-5c31-b599-52fb25ac49a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "847972c0-6f90-54f3-90b0-10ccc1176f30" :question/math-academy-id "q-159299" :question/problem "$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & b \\\\ c & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe covariance matrix $Σ$ for some random variables ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ is given above. Find $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "For random variables ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n},$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{n}] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Var}[{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is ${Σ}_{ii}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ and\n\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i\\ne j$ is ${Σ}_{ij}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a={Σ}_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}]={Σ}_{21}=1$\n\n- $b={Σ}_{23}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{3},{X}_{2}]={Σ}_{32}=1$\n\n- $c={Σ}_{31}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{3},{X}_{1}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{3}]={Σ}_{13}=0$\n\nTherefore, $a+b+c=1+1+0=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37d7e5c8-9b77-5eb1-beb8-4355a5a9d625" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Elements of a Covariance Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb83ca6-c5af-599a-b4a2-ce490e201e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547346} {:answer/id #uuid "45514e1f-281c-598d-9509-50a74cbf7a87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547469} {:answer/id #uuid "8d59eb94-f7fb-5092-8e24-c25a0ebe9daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547632} {:answer/id #uuid "f0618289-dca3-5dc0-bcbd-4eac5be4c5a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547810} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a35b08-5cdb-5fb6-b020-87e2fc8342e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45514e1f-281c-598d-9509-50a74cbf7a87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547469} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a904e50-7e60-5751-ab67-cc77c08ff21a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc3c12f1-dbf7-51f0-875a-0336c3cabe74" :question/math-academy-id "q-159300" :question/problem "$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ a & 2 & b \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe covariance matrix $Σ$ for some random variables ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ is given above. Find $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "For random variables ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n},$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{n}] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Var}[{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is ${Σ}_{ii}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ and\n\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i\\ne j$ is ${Σ}_{ij}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a={Σ}_{21}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]={Σ}_{12}=1$\n\n- $b={Σ}_{23}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{3},{X}_{2}]={Σ}_{32}=0$\n\nTherefore, $a+b=1+0=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37d7e5c8-9b77-5eb1-beb8-4355a5a9d625" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Elements of a Covariance Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "179ae555-0cf8-5046-839f-e2bb315ad66a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547365} {:answer/id #uuid "17d439c6-f9f2-509a-aa62-86fe12e95d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547366} {:answer/id #uuid "673712b1-3e53-5bb6-b346-1759844e58b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547545} {:answer/id #uuid "7684fde1-d33b-56b3-bcf1-2e573af3f336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547574} {:answer/id #uuid "cc935f2d-69c1-55c8-92f7-b1855d61503e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc935f2d-69c1-55c8-92f7-b1855d61503e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547750} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fadab62-7bc0-5380-9887-85d299772dbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dcf740fc-84e9-5215-a4db-974afa421f20" :question/math-academy-id "q-159302" :question/problem "$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ a & b & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe covariance matrix $Σ$ for some random variables ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ is given above. Find $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "For random variables ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n},$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{n}] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Var}[{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is ${Σ}_{ii}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ and\n\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i\\ne j$ is ${Σ}_{ij}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a={Σ}_{31}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{3},{X}_{1}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{3}]={Σ}_{13}=0$\n\n- $b={Σ}_{32}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{3},{X}_{2}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]={Σ}_{23}=2$\n\nTherefore, $a+b=0+2=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37d7e5c8-9b77-5eb1-beb8-4355a5a9d625" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Elements of a Covariance Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29b8007a-7576-50c7-a941-bfc6800ec82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547395} {:answer/id #uuid "552bfb8d-466d-54ab-9d47-28aa839de529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547506} {:answer/id #uuid "66edc399-fbce-5668-b0c8-4afdbacf1e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547544} {:answer/id #uuid "7e737b3e-2f18-53be-a811-3a02e6d46165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547589} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6df6eb-25d5-5f0c-8b5c-7016a410eedf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e737b3e-2f18-53be-a811-3a02e6d46165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547589} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5e9fda2-d0c9-5617-8daa-b5eb8702fda8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89c1f4fa-b7a3-52ff-a3e0-636f4aa1573d" :question/math-academy-id "q-159304" :question/problem "$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & a \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe covariance matrix $Σ$ for some random variables${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ is given above. Find $a.$" :question/worked-solution "For random variables ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n},$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{n}] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Var}[{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is ${Σ}_{ii}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ and\n\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i\\ne j$ is ${Σ}_{ij}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a={Σ}_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}]={Σ}_{21}=1$\n\nTherefore, $a=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37d7e5c8-9b77-5eb1-beb8-4355a5a9d625" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Elements of a Covariance Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2921d166-d921-581c-9b9a-37d5f327bf17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547392} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc96003-4d3d-5775-99b8-1aa90fcfc7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547488} {:answer/id #uuid "610030e0-629d-52b1-8c62-4d3373d13fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547528} {:answer/id #uuid "800973ee-5a75-5845-8cc8-fdeeef80ea74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547590} {:answer/id #uuid "acb77e59-b789-5c45-a661-4f088cb01c70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bc96003-4d3d-5775-99b8-1aa90fcfc7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547488} :answer-field/id #uuid "74a4e5cf-b19c-51b8-aa94-11f87242a385" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef075455-3a78-5d07-aadc-6948b6898570" :question/math-academy-id "q-159309" :question/problem "$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & a \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe covariance matrix $Σ$ for some random variables ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ is given above. Find $a.$" :question/worked-solution "For random variables ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n},$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{n}] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Var}[{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is ${Σ}_{ii}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ and\n\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i\\ne j$ is ${Σ}_{ij}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a={Σ}_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}]={Σ}_{21}=3$\n\nTherefore, $a=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548018 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2c2ed97-706b-565d-997e-39bd0c832726" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given Some Moments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a451da81-2342-5e07-a6f8-5dec64943041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547677} {:answer/id #uuid "a562d998-1234-5593-adcf-b17837715b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 40 \\\\ 40 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547679} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a2bba6-91a4-580c-9f2a-a7c46f1f9ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547707} {:answer/id #uuid "c4706484-e47e-53d7-916e-7f3834b2f527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547740} {:answer/id #uuid "cddd1084-3169-5e4a-8361-489a8477cc24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4706484-e47e-53d7-916e-7f3834b2f527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547740} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c168029-f889-5fc1-8200-9cf79199ce99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72d6fd40-f077-555f-a2ea-48615443e754" :question/math-academy-id "q-159366" :question/problem "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\text{E}[X]=6,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=7,$ mixed moment $\\text{E}[XY]=40,$ and variances $\\text{Var}[X]=2$ and $\\text{Var}[Y]=5.$ Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nUsing the above formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =40-6\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2c2ed97-706b-565d-997e-39bd0c832726" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given Some Moments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5239a141-bf7d-5658-bb2f-df63fbdbb88d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547502} {:answer/id #uuid "5573dd6f-f3e4-550f-872e-c2f443e9bdd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547507} {:answer/id #uuid "abdceee3-f8fd-5a9a-b7f6-4ee239047601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547692} {:answer/id #uuid "be5bed0d-e0c3-5858-ad2d-ed53ca98ebad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -36 \\\\ -36 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547720} {:answer/id #uuid "eb663fe2-f592-5971-9d46-f608f6220c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5573dd6f-f3e4-550f-872e-c2f443e9bdd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547507} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b1bad76-5478-5037-a22f-95c8d75dbbe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cae81e4a-bed5-56e8-841e-be25ff76e753" :question/math-academy-id "q-159369" :question/problem "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\text{E}[X]=3,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=1,$ mixed moment $\\text{E}[XY]=4,$ and second raw moments $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=10$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=4.$ Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\text{Var}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =10-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =4-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow lets find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =4-3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2c2ed97-706b-565d-997e-39bd0c832726" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given Some Moments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "500cd04e-121c-5ac9-8280-695271e27690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547498} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed049c1-daad-5bec-907a-225c42ecb2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547634} {:answer/id #uuid "90739cf3-46a8-5d3a-a97b-8598afc71c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547637} {:answer/id #uuid "94938d70-fccc-53b2-81dc-000bf63ebcbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547644} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a64a65-b8ad-5c0f-87ad-0b1a06c87250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90739cf3-46a8-5d3a-a97b-8598afc71c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547637} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e674815-858b-557f-9bb3-eda2dd10d5bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea8dc2eb-77b6-5a6b-972c-806dd20059a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-159371" :question/problem "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\text{E}[X]=2,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=3,$ mixed moment $\\text{E}[XY]=6,$ and second raw moments $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=9$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=17.$ Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\text{Var}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =9-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =17-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow lets find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =6-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2c2ed97-706b-565d-997e-39bd0c832726" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given Some Moments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039aa489-bcf6-5182-bbed-08d284bdcb3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547324} {:answer/id #uuid "25d7cc4e-69e8-52ab-a7db-2e02b18378c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547387} {:answer/id #uuid "2f570de4-595d-57a4-817e-0177624b7327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547412} {:answer/id #uuid "858e8b64-89a3-525e-a632-86d0e4430fb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547603} {:answer/id #uuid "a1702269-bbef-5047-bac9-8bafb421d755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f570de4-595d-57a4-817e-0177624b7327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547412} :answer-field/id #uuid "633f0d3c-8b4a-54a7-a87f-04b8012968de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29dbfdc5-a896-5333-926c-2c4e2687cdd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-159373" :question/problem "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\text{E}[X]=3,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=5,$ mixed moment $\\text{E}[XY]=10,$ and second raw moments $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=16$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=31.$ Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\text{Var}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =16-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =31-{5}^{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow lets find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =10-3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46118198-5e82-5b8f-8adc-6f25ecbd9b3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given a Discrete Bivariate Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e5010e5-3f4c-5ae6-a631-444f758a8718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 2.16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547409} {:answer/id #uuid "59ebd30d-4e1e-5504-b5d3-d789ce481c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -0.6 \\\\ -0.6 & 2.16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547514} {:answer/id #uuid "6c61b5ff-f207-5b0b-8365-c3c4253c6256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1.08 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.27\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547556} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9420af-2ceb-5c7e-8f61-b22aa3d7ebde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1.08 & -0.6 \\\\ -0.6 & 0.27\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547661} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3a169b-897a-5f61-b729-9f40a0af1f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -0.4 \\\\ -0.4 & 1.18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59ebd30d-4e1e-5504-b5d3-d789ce481c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -0.6 \\\\ -0.6 & 2.16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547514} :answer-field/id #uuid "04216e6b-fcd6-5776-bcd4-b6b33f9033e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c722f59-6d05-5224-b152-d5620b69f9be" :question/math-academy-id "q-159392" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ |\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=2$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=1.2,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=3$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.6+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =3.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =3.6-{1.2}^{2} \\\\  & =2.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1)(0)f(1,0)+(3)(0)f(3,0)+(1)(3)f(1,3)+(3)(3)f(3,3) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.2+0\\cdot 0.4+3\\cdot 0.3+9\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =1.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1.8-2\\cdot 1.2 \\\\  & =-0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -0.6 \\\\ -0.6 & 2.16\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46118198-5e82-5b8f-8adc-6f25ecbd9b3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given a Discrete Bivariate Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2073e760-f9ee-5b53-8058-7e137e9dedaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.17 & -0.05 \\\\ -0.05 & 0.42\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547380} {:answer/id #uuid "44f91ee6-4be9-543a-b041-f7c5cfc18615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.21 & -0.02 \\\\ -0.02 & 0.24\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547468} {:answer/id #uuid "615faddf-3bab-5914-9173-a556bc356111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.15 & 0.04 \\\\ 0.04 & 0.33\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547529} {:answer/id #uuid "89bee81d-2596-5647-822b-2c6a80c7ab59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.15 & -0.02 \\\\ -0.02 & 0.33\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547614} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c8c0e5-04d4-547e-ab87-e1edff505050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.21 & 0.04 \\\\ 0.04 & 0.24\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44f91ee6-4be9-543a-b041-f7c5cfc18615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.21 & -0.02 \\\\ -0.02 & 0.24\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547468} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8af4fef-3d5d-5433-ae27-4591c3245789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0df8fdd-91c9-54e0-8d8a-27e731147330" :question/math-academy-id "q-159394" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=1.7$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=0.6,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.7$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.3+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.7 \\\\  & =3.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =3.1-{1.7}^{2} \\\\  & =0.21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{1}^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =0.6-{0.6}^{2} \\\\  & =0.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1)(0)f(1,0)+(2)(0)f(2,0)+(1)(1)f(1,1)+(2)(1)f(2,1) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.1+0\\cdot 0.3+1\\cdot 0.2+2\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1-1.7\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =-0.02.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.21 & -0.02 \\\\ -0.02 & 0.24\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46118198-5e82-5b8f-8adc-6f25ecbd9b3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given a Discrete Bivariate Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157318a2-618f-5f21-930e-43f75d5b5084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -0.4 \\\\ -0.4 & 4.3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547361} {:answer/id #uuid "28a84dfd-6aa2-5495-a64d-69de32523f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.32 & -1.4 \\\\ -1.4 & 0.16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547389} {:answer/id #uuid "3967486f-6093-5d20-8dc9-663e628dffd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -0.1 \\\\ -0.1 & 4.3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547437} {:answer/id #uuid "62b6f626-2881-5d24-a9b7-ba0b307eee05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1.4 \\\\ -1.4 & 0.91\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547532} {:answer/id #uuid "cda86d07-4c70-5b02-bb43-601bb10cfd6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -0.1 \\\\ -0.1 & 0.91\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cda86d07-4c70-5b02-bb43-601bb10cfd6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -0.1 \\\\ -0.1 & 0.91\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547755} :answer-field/id #uuid "2db09cf4-b474-5e57-9f08-283223d0e1e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f655f9f5-f0a0-522e-aa7b-2faf26b5e6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-159396" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ |\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=5$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=2.7,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ | $0.75$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.65$ | $0.35$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+{6}^{2}\\cdot 0.75 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =28-{5}^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0.65+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.35 \\\\  & =8.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =8.2-{2.7}^{2} \\\\  & =0.91.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2)(2)f(2,2)+(6)(2)f(6,2)+(2)(4)f(2,4)+(6)(4)f(6,4) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.15+12\\cdot 0.5+8\\cdot 0.1+24\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =13.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =13.4-5\\cdot 2.7 \\\\  & =-0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -0.1 \\\\ -0.1 & 0.91\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46118198-5e82-5b8f-8adc-6f25ecbd9b3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given a Discrete Bivariate Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0030f44f-ba08-5c74-8bf5-6a609de1c50f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1.14 & 0.02 \\\\ 0.02 & 0.72\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547315} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6c42a0-cab8-5788-bf15-3a5dd81f1e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.24 & 0.02 \\\\ 0.02 & 0.16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547497} {:answer/id #uuid "657cb2f7-bdcc-52c1-a370-bf6419c7cb74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2.6 & 1.14 \\\\ 1.14 & 1.8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547540} {:answer/id #uuid "743641c5-77ba-546d-98c6-ead83cff1a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2.6 & 1.4 \\\\ 1.4 & 1.8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547571} {:answer/id #uuid "8c572c21-35a0-57e5-bb60-83f696067df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.24 & 1.4 \\\\ 1.4 & 0.16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f6c42a0-cab8-5788-bf15-3a5dd81f1e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.24 & 0.02 \\\\ 0.02 & 0.16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547497} :answer-field/id #uuid "e947e310-d910-5ad9-bf8c-eecd137f0212" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67d9ef63-b4c5-5251-99ba-45e5490b628a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159401" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ |\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=1.6$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=0.8,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =2.8-{1.6}^{2} \\\\  & =0.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{1}^{2}\\cdot 0.8 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =0.8-{0.8}^{2} \\\\  & =0.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1)(0)f(1,0)+(2)(0)f(2,0)+(1)(1)f(1,1)+(2)(1)f(2,1) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.1+0\\cdot 0.1+1\\cdot 0.3+2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1.3-1.6\\cdot 0.8 \\\\  & =0.02.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.24 & 0.02 \\\\ 0.02 & 0.16\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188364 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e65da0e-4de3-5292-aaef-c646771ec0f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given a Continuous Bivariate Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04876404-2581-5642-a614-ac8532d94ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{51}{80}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547326} {:answer/id #uuid "28e7f859-7126-5457-a4e0-c1a1e1d2204b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{11}{40}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547390} {:answer/id #uuid "32e2ee64-527c-5c83-b039-b6ea6f47e219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{23}{40}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547422} {:answer/id #uuid "f06f1240-944d-5a7e-801d-c1580106d19c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{23}{40}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547811} {:answer/id #uuid "f530e59e-19da-56aa-a5f6-c0afac7fd1bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{11}{40}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04876404-2581-5642-a614-ac8532d94ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{51}{80}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547326} :answer-field/id #uuid "3653cd9d-fe12-51a1-8be3-fd825c0586b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53e9c56c-8467-523e-bd98-6ac7236cb57e" :question/math-academy-id "q-159402" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\, & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,-1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=0$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{5}{4},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{4}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-{(0)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{33}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5}-{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{80}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$ The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle\n\n$$\nS=[-1,1]\\times [-1,2].\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=g(x)\\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}$ and $h(y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}.$\n\nHence, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{51}{80}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188362 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46118198-5e82-5b8f-8adc-6f25ecbd9b3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given a Discrete Bivariate Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1475ee74-c0d3-5ee6-aa81-f0f5cdcab5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.16 & 0.07 \\\\ 0.07 & 0.24\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547357} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc5a00d-83e6-59b0-b208-3d137720bd47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.16 & 0.14 \\\\ 0.14 & 0.24\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547565} {:answer/id #uuid "71264c49-74b7-50ea-bc44-c7d200e335a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.12 & 0.21 \\\\ 0.21 & 0.28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547568} {:answer/id #uuid "793608fa-e9eb-5e5a-8e83-288dd037a5f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.07 & 0.21 \\\\ 0.21 & 0.14\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547579} {:answer/id #uuid "9f252122-7a78-5806-a3f7-44a4c28dc4f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.12 & 0.07 \\\\ 0.07 & 0.28\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1475ee74-c0d3-5ee6-aa81-f0f5cdcab5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.16 & 0.07 \\\\ 0.07 & 0.24\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547357} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7d1b7f0-9375-5f6f-bc52-a79347709299" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "498cfd6b-9969-58ee-9468-55dacd0e9142" :question/math-academy-id "q-159406" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.55$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=2.2$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=1.4,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.55$ | $0.25$ | $0.8$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0.8+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5-{2.2}^{2} \\\\  & =0.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.6+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =2.2-{1.4}^{2} \\\\  & =0.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2)(1)f(2,1)+(3)(1)f(3,1)+(2)(2)f(2,2)+(3)(2)f(3,2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.55+3\\cdot 0.05+4\\cdot 0.25+6\\cdot 0.15 \\\\  & =3.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =3.15-2.2\\cdot 1.4 \\\\  & =0.07.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.16 & 0.07 \\\\ 0.07 & 0.24\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188364 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e65da0e-4de3-5292-aaef-c646771ec0f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given a Continuous Bivariate Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34950a5f-0e45-502b-a869-e016971bd6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{8}{75}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547425} {:answer/id #uuid "4c028ae0-4e46-5185-bea4-b79e305dc544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{8}{75}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547489} {:answer/id #uuid "4dfa5556-a2dc-5232-9d7f-e3796a0407dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9} & -1 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{8}{75}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547494} {:answer/id #uuid "8675456c-15df-5d06-a100-2d7b178a0c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{25}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547606} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc276fa-92de-5c9e-be25-aa6ec3df6cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18} & -1 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{4}{25}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34950a5f-0e45-502b-a869-e016971bd6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{8}{75}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547425} :answer-field/id #uuid "294a6c0d-e8c4-539b-87eb-6c2be022c57a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9895539f-08a9-52d3-aba6-0550c7a4d108" :question/math-academy-id "q-159407" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{3},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{8}{5},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{3})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{3})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{5}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{6}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}-{(\\frac{8}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$ The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle\n\n$$\nS=[0,1]\\times [0,2].\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=g(x)\\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)=2x$ and $h(y)=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}.$\n\nHence, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{8}{75}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188364 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e65da0e-4de3-5292-aaef-c646771ec0f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given a Continuous Bivariate Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66021cb4-d4dc-5536-b740-cb112f549955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{25} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{1}{80}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547542} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab77c8f-f65f-5385-b65d-d3ff9200abe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{75} & \\frac{1}{120} \\\\ \\frac{1}{120} & \\frac{1}{80}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547618} {:answer/id #uuid "8bea8944-aeb8-56ab-bc44-a9115f9d6b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{75} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547621} {:answer/id #uuid "c0675adb-146f-509c-9662-380def9fc9b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{50} & \\frac{1}{120} \\\\ \\frac{1}{120} & \\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547730} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4fcec9-3717-5441-ad16-3b9193cd5316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{75} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{3}{20}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ab77c8f-f65f-5385-b65d-d3ff9200abe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{75} & \\frac{1}{120} \\\\ \\frac{1}{120} & \\frac{1}{80}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547618} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcc58af9-a17d-54ac-a6d0-c02b4698e66a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f28747a-e241-5f89-b726-699a85c63f84" :question/math-academy-id "q-159419" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}48y(x-y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{4}{5},$ $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1}{4},$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{2}{3},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\text{Var}[X].$ Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}48{y}^{3}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}(x{y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{y=0}^{y=x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{64}{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{160}{x}^{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48\\cdot \\frac{3}{320}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{20}{[\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{40}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{80}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}xy\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}48x{y}^{2}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-x{y}^{3})\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{64}{x}^{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48\\cdot \\frac{5}{192}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{[\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}-(\\frac{4}{5})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{120}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{75} & \\frac{1}{120} \\\\ \\frac{1}{120} & \\frac{1}{80}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188364 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e65da0e-4de3-5292-aaef-c646771ec0f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Covariance Matrix Given a Continuous Bivariate Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c09fbcf-e1dc-5950-9508-6766eb430f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{45} & \\frac{5}{63} \\\\ \\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{67}{548}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547447} {:answer/id #uuid "8486b2b4-5627-54ae-9bfc-12a68a4ca561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{1}{21} \\\\ \\frac{1}{21} & \\frac{56}{843}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547600} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3456dc-3ee8-5bd4-b669-70e0e8adddde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{21} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{760}{1\\,323}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547753} {:answer/id #uuid "ce082f30-4d9c-529e-8724-6a216a95f170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{5}{63} \\\\ \\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{760}{1\\,323}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547758} {:answer/id #uuid "ff923f25-09d1-59df-bac6-3e88c64bc811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{45} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{56}{843}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce082f30-4d9c-529e-8724-6a216a95f170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{5}{63} \\\\ \\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{760}{1\\,323}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547758} :answer-field/id #uuid "3850f8d6-6285-5466-abaf-4c01feeefec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7039fb00-3d4a-54d6-a4b0-1bcf58d49ef2" :question/math-academy-id "q-159422" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{16}({x}^{2}-y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{5}{3},$ $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{20}{21},$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=\\frac{40}{27},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\text{Var}[X].$\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\frac{5}{16}{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}({x}^{4}-y{x}^{2})\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[y{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{x}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{32}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{6}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{32}{[\\frac{1}{7}{x}^{7}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{7}-{(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{63}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{Var}[Y].$ Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{27}-{(\\frac{20}{21})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{760}{1\\,323}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}xy\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\frac{5}{16}xy({x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}x]}_{y=0}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{7})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{7}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{96}{[\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{8}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}-(\\frac{5}{3})\\cdot (\\frac{20}{21}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{63}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{5}{63} \\\\ \\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{760}{1\\,323}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}]}
